# Register {{ name }}

`make new-service` cannot finish the job. `tach.toml` and `.importlinter` are human-owned
guardrails (A15): the agent hooks block edits to them, so a human pastes those snippets and
CODEOWNERS reviews them. `compose/services.yml`, `.env.example` and `docs/CONTEXT-MAP.md`
are ordinary files an agent may edit. Until the boundary snippets are in, `make check` fails.
`sb-graph check` checks tach `source_roots`, import-linter `root_packages`, pyright `include`,
pytest `testpaths` and complete canonical service contracts: CLEAN layers,
pure cores, library/service direction, test-only imports and service independence. Partial
contracts and import exemptions fail; import-linter separately checks actual dependencies.
Include every current non-kernel library in pure-core restrictions, extending the snippet below
if the workspace has gained libraries. OpenAPI service discovery is automatic.
Delete this file once everything is pasted and `make check` passes.

### tach.toml

Append `"services/{{ name }}/src"` to `source_roots`:

```toml
"services/{{ name }}/src"
```

Then add the module:

```toml
[[modules]]
path = "{{ name }}"
# The platform libraries are separate distributions: enforced by `tach check-external`.
depends_on = []
```

### .importlinter

Add `{{ name }}` to `root_packages` (in `[importlinter]`) and to the `source_modules` of
`testing-only-in-tests`. For A10, add `{{ name }}` to `forbidden_modules` of `libs-never-import-services`.
Then append these contracts:

```ini
[importlinter:contract:{{ name }}-layers]
name = {{ name }} CLEAN layers (A1)
type = layers
containers =
    {{ name }}
layers =
    main | (worker) | (consumer) | (seed)
    bootstrap | migrate
    interface
    infrastructure
    application
    domain

[importlinter:contract:{{ name }}-pure-core]
name = {{ name }} domain/application import no frameworks or adapters (A2)
type = forbidden
source_modules =
    {{ name }}.domain
    {{ name }}.application
forbidden_modules =
    sqlalchemy
    fastapi
    starlette
    sqladmin
    temporalio
    confluent_kafka
    httpx
    pydantic_settings
    structlog
    sb_db
    sb_messaging
    sb_workflows
    sb_http
    sb_admin
    sb_obs
    sb_testing
```

### Services never import services

A9, in `.importlinter`.
{% if not peers.split() -%}
This is the first service: nothing to add yet. The next service's `REGISTER.md` creates the
`services-independent` contract.
{% elif independence_contract -%}
The contract exists: add `{{ name }}` to `modules` of `services-independent`:

```ini
    {{ name }}
```
{% else -%}
Append this contract (it lists every existing service and `{{ name }}`):

```ini
[importlinter:contract:services-independent]
name = Services never import each other (A9)
type = independence
modules =
{%- for peer in peers.split() %}
    {{ peer }}
{%- endfor %}
    {{ name }}
```
{% endif %}
### pyrightconfig.json and pyproject.toml

Nothing to paste: pyright's `include` has `services`, its `strict` globs
(`services/*/src/*/domain`, `services/*/src/*/application`) already cover this service, and
pytest's `testpaths` has `services`.

### compose/services.yml

Paste these anchors above `services:`:

```yaml
x-{{ name }}-build: &{{ name }}-build
  context: ..
  dockerfile: services/{{ name }}/Dockerfile
  target: dev
  args: {SERVICE: {{ name }}}

x-{{ name }}-environment: &{{ name }}-environment
  SERVICE: {{ name }}
  # Required, no defaults (D1): dev auth and the admin exist only in local/dev/test/ci.
  {{ NAME }}_ENVIRONMENT: dev
  {{ NAME }}_AUTH_MODE: ${SB_AUTH_MODE:?set SB_AUTH_MODE in .env (see .env.example)}
  # The app processes get only the sb_app URL (RLS applies); the privileged URLs are given to
  # {{ name }}-migrate alone.
  {{ NAME }}_DATABASE_URL: postgresql+asyncpg://sb_app:${SB_APP_PASSWORD:?set SB_APP_PASSWORD in .env (see .env.example)}@postgres:5432/{{ name }}
  {{ NAME }}_DATABASE_NAME: {{ name }}
  {{ NAME }}_KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP: kafka:29092
  {{ NAME }}_TEMPORAL_TARGET: temporal:7233
  {{ NAME }}_TEMPORAL_NAMESPACE: default
  {{ NAME }}_TASK_QUEUE: {{ name }}
  {{ NAME }}_LOG_LEVEL: INFO

x-{{ name }}-volumes: &{{ name }}-volumes
  - ../services/{{ name }}/src:/repo/services/{{ name }}/src
  - ../services/{{ name }}/migrations:/repo/services/{{ name }}/migrations
  - ../services/{{ name }}/alembic.ini:/repo/services/{{ name }}/alembic.ini:ro
  - ../libs/py:/repo/libs/py
```

and these under `services:` (the `x-infra-healthy` anchor already exists):

```yaml
  {{ name }}-migrate:
    profiles: ["{{ name }}-migrate"]
    build: *{{ name }}-build
    environment:
      <<: *{{ name }}-environment
      # The only process given the BYPASSRLS migrator and the database-creating admin URLs.
      {{ NAME }}_MIGRATOR_URL: postgresql+asyncpg://sb_migrator:${SB_MIGRATOR_PASSWORD:?set SB_MIGRATOR_PASSWORD in .env (see .env.example)}@postgres:5432/{{ name }}
      {{ NAME }}_ADMIN_DATABASE_URL: postgresql+asyncpg://sb_admin:${SB_PG_ADMIN_PASSWORD:?set SB_PG_ADMIN_PASSWORD in .env (see .env.example)}@postgres:5432/postgres
    volumes: *{{ name }}-volumes
    command: ["python", "-m", "{{ name }}.migrate"]
    depends_on:
      postgres: {condition: service_healthy}

  {{ name }}-api:
    profiles: ["{{ name }}"]
    build: *{{ name }}-build
    environment:
      <<: *{{ name }}-environment
      # D8: SB_ADMIN_* map to {{ NAME }}_ADMIN_*; the secret must be at least 32 characters.
      # Only the API mounts the admin, so only it gets them (build_api fails fast without).
      {{ NAME }}_ADMIN_USERNAME: ${SB_ADMIN_USERNAME:?set SB_ADMIN_USERNAME in .env (see .env.example)}
      {{ NAME }}_ADMIN_PASSWORD: ${SB_ADMIN_PASSWORD:?set SB_ADMIN_PASSWORD in .env (see .env.example)}
      {{ NAME }}_ADMIN_SECRET_KEY: ${SB_ADMIN_SECRET_KEY:?set SB_ADMIN_SECRET_KEY (32+ chars) in .env (see .env.example)}
    volumes: *{{ name }}-volumes
    # uvicorn with log_config=None and reload dirs services/{{ name }}/src and libs/py (D19).
    command: ["python", "-m", "{{ name }}.main", "--reload"]
    ports: ["127.0.0.1:${SB_{{ NAME }}_API_PORT:-{{ api_port }}}:8000"]
    depends_on: *infra-healthy
    healthcheck:
      test:
        - CMD
        - python
        - -c
        - "import urllib.request,sys; sys.exit(0 if urllib.request.urlopen('http://localhost:8000/health/ready', timeout=3).status==200 else 1)"
      interval: 3s
      timeout: 5s
      retries: 40
      start_period: 10s
```

A worker or consumer process is added with its seam (the `temporal-workflow` and `kafka-event`
skills), in the same shape as the reference service's. Add `SB_{{ NAME }}_API_PORT={{ api_port }}`
to `.env.example` next to the other ports.

Port {{ api_port }} is `NEXT_API_PORT` in `mk/scaffold.mk`: one above the highest API port
already in `compose/services.yml`. Two services generated before either is registered get the
same port; give the second one the next number here and in its `CLAUDE.md`.

### OpenAPI

Service discovery uses the workspace graph. Run `make openapi` and commit
`contracts/openapi/{{ name }}.json`.

### docs/CONTEXT-MAP.md

Add a row for `{{ name }}` pointing at `services/{{ name }}/CONTEXT.md`.

### Then

```sh
uv sync --all-packages
make graph
make check
```

Commit `docs/generated/` and `uv.lock` with the service. Kubernetes manifests
(`deploy/k8s/{{ kebab }}/`) come before the first deploy: copy the reference service's tree and
rename its names and `{{ NAME }}_*` variables. Kubernetes names must be DNS labels (no `_`), so
the k8s directory, every resource name and the image use the hyphenated form `{{ kebab }}`
(`{{ kebab }}-api`, `{{ kebab }}-migrate`, `{{ kebab }}-config`,
`ghcr.io/<owner>/dark-factory-{{ kebab }}`, label `app.kubernetes.io/name: {{ kebab }}`), while the
Python package, the service directory, the compose names and the env prefix keep `{{ name }}`.
`images.yml` builds `services/{{ name }}/Dockerfile` with `SERVICE={{ name }}`; `deploy.yml`
accepts `{{ name }}` or `{{ kebab }}` and deploys `deploy/k8s/{{ kebab }}/`.
